  <content>
  <section><title>Lesson Narrative</title>
<para>In earlier lessons, students factored the standard form of a quadratic function to find its solutions, or zeros.
  Students will now be provided the zeros of a quadratic function and convert them into the factored form and standard
  form of that function.</para>
<para>Students will also distinguish between finding a quadratic function that has certain zeros and finding the specific
  quadratic function that has certain zeros and passes through a particular point. They will learn to differentiate
  between these related quadratic functions.</para>
<title>Learning Goals (Teacher Facing)</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> Convert fluently between a quadratic function's zeros, its factored form, and its standard form. </item>
  <item> Identify the zeros on the graph of a quadratic function and use them to write the standard form of that function.
  </item>
  <item> Use the intercept form of a quadratic function, \(f(x) = a(x − p)(x − q)\), to define a specific
    function given its zeros and a point. </item>
</list>
<title>Learning Targets (Student Facing) </title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> Find the standard form of a quadratic function given its zeros. </item>
  <item> Find the standard form of a quadratic function given its graph. </item>
  <item> Use the intercept form of a quadratic function to define a specific function given its zeros and a point. </item>
</list>


